---
import { withBase } from "../../lib/paths";
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { cases } from "../../data/profile";
const item = cases.find((caseItem) => caseItem.slug === "apit")!;
---
<BaseLayout title="APIT" description="APIT AI capstone project case study." >
  <section class="border-b border-[var(--border-subtle)] pb-16 pt-28 md:pb-24 md:pt-36">
    <div class="site-shell">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-6">
        <span class="rounded-md border border-sky-500/20 bg-sky-500/10 px-2.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-semibold text-sky-700 dark:border-sky-400/30 dark:bg-sky-400/15 dark:text-sky-300">{item.label}</span>
        {item.period && <span class="tag">{item.period}</span>}
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-[var(--text-base)] mb-6 leading-tight max-w-4xl">APIT: Agent Programmatic Integration Testing.</h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-[var(--text-muted)] max-w-3xl leading-relaxed mb-6">{item.summary}</p>
      <div class="p-5 bg-sky-500/10 border border-sky-500/20 rounded-xl mb-8 max-w-3xl flex flex-col gap-1">
        <strong class="text-sky-800 dark:text-sky-300 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M9 18h6"/><path d="M10 22h4"/><path d="M12 2v1"/><path d="M12 7a5 5 0 0 0-5 5c0 2 1.5 3 2.5 4h5c1-1 2.5-2 2.5-4a5 5 0 0 0-5-5Z"/></svg>
          What's in the name?
        </strong>
        <span class="text-[var(--text-muted)] text-sm leading-relaxed mt-1">The project name is a strategic play on words: while the immediate scope executes <strong class="text-[var(--text-base)]">API Testing</strong>, the system architecture establishes a foundation for <strong class="text-[var(--text-base)]">Agent Programmatic Integration Testing</strong>.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-4">
        {item.docsUrl && <a class="btn-primary" href={withBase(item.docsUrl)} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open capstone PDF</a>}
        <a class="btn-secondary" href={withBase("projects/")}>Back to projects</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="py-24">
    <div class="case-study-shell">
      <article class="case-article">
        <section>
          <h2 class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-sky-700 dark:text-sky-400 mb-4">Problem</h2>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--text-base)] mb-6">Manual API test design does not scale.</h3>
          <p class="text-[var(--text-muted)] leading-relaxed mb-6">Execution tools like Postman and JMeter still depend on manually designed test cases. APIT demonstrates LLM-based test generation: it reads API documentation, defines test scope, generates structured test cases, executes them, and reports pass/fail outcomes.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-sky-700 dark:text-sky-400 mb-4">Solution architecture</h2>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--text-base)] mb-6">A controlled model lifecycle from domain data to deployable inference.</h3>
          <p class="text-[var(--text-muted)] leading-relaxed mb-6">The solution treats test generation as a full model-and-system problem rather than a prompt-only feature. API documents were distilled into prompts, used to generate 1,258 bilingual English/Vietnamese samples, and converted into structured test-case outputs with reasoning. Qwen-2.5-3B and Llama-3.2-3B were fine-tuned with LoRA/QLoRA under controlled settings, then the selected model was served through an OpenAI-compatible vLLM endpoint on RunPod for application integration.</p>
          <div class="diagram">
            <div class="diagram-step"><b>Document preprocessing</b><span>Standardize API docs from OpenAPI, Swagger, PDF, or structured text.</span></div>
            <div class="diagram-step"><b>Test scope</b><span>Select requirements, endpoints, and value/risk boundaries for generation.</span></div>
            <div class="diagram-step"><b>Model adaptation</b><span>Synthetic data + LoRA/QLoRA adapt a small model to structured API-test generation.</span></div>
            <div class="diagram-step"><b>Serving & execution</b><span>OpenAI-compatible vLLM inference feeds test execution, validation, and reporting.</span></div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-sky-700 dark:text-sky-400 mb-4">Evaluation</h2>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--text-base)] mb-6">Structured output quality measured with reference-based metrics.</h3>
          <p class="text-[var(--text-muted)] leading-relaxed mb-6">Generated test cases were evaluated with precision, recall, and F1 using fuzzy string similarity, numeric tolerance, and categorical matching. Qwen-2.5-3B LoRA/QLoRA achieved 0.655 macro-F1, a 41.5% relative improvement over the Llama-3.2-3B baseline.</p>
          <p class="text-[var(--text-muted)] leading-relaxed mb-6">Reasoning quality was assessed through logical validity, coherence, and groundedness, aligning with the agent evaluation direction.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-sky-700 dark:text-sky-400 mb-4">Agent Testing Connection</h2>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--text-base)] mb-6">APIT as evidence for the Agent Testing / Evaluation direction.</h3>
          <p class="text-[var(--text-muted)] leading-relaxed mb-6">APIT and <a href={withBase("projects/ata/")}>Agent Assurance</a> address different reliability questions. APIT evaluates a fine-tuned model's structured API-test generation; Agent Assurance examines observable agent execution and quality gates. Both rely on explicit evaluation criteria, but their metrics and execution contracts are not interchangeable.</p>
          <p class="text-[var(--text-muted)] leading-relaxed mb-6">APIT's documented result is model-level performance on structured test-case generation, not a claim of improved production API-test coverage or measured agent reliability. Its practical contribution is a reproducible data → fine-tuning → serving → evaluation workflow.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-sky-700 dark:text-sky-400 mb-4">Stack</h2>
          <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mt-4">
            {item.tags.map((tag) => <span class="tag">{tag}</span>)}
          </div>
        </section>
      </article>

      <aside class="case-aside">
        <div class="evidence-card">
          <h3 class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-sky-700 dark:text-sky-400 mb-4">Result</h3>
          <p class="text-[var(--text-muted)] leading-relaxed mb-6"><strong class="text-[var(--text-base)]">0.655 macro-F1</strong> on structured API test generation, plus a deployable OpenAI-compatible vLLM inference service on RunPod.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="evidence-card">
          <h3 class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-sky-700 dark:text-sky-400 mb-4">Spec</h3>
          <dl class="spec-list">
            <div><dt>Role</dt><dd>{item.role}</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Dataset</dt><dd>1,258 samples</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Models</dt><dd>Qwen-2.5-3B, Llama-3.2-3B</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Method</dt><dd>LoRA/QLoRA · 4-bit training</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Serving</dt><dd>vLLM · RunPod</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
